1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
 We end our course with dying, which of course is the end of life.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:19,000
 When we talk about dying and death, one thing we have to understand is that we look at it culturally very different.

3
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:26,000
 Basically, in some cases we use death as an analogy for something, or something that's good or bad.

4
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:34,000
 But we do find that death and bereavement and all those aspects tend to be very different from culture to culture.

5
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:42,000
 As you see in the pictures down here, we have three very different ways we celebrate perhaps, or we show about death.

6
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,000
 So the first one is sort of a traditional New Orleans Jazz Festival.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,000
 The second one you may not have heard about, it's kind of fun to look up.

8
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:56,000
 In Ghana, the people have customized coffins made that represent a lot of who they are throughout their lifetime.

9
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,000
 So in this case, it was a fisherman who had that coffin made.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
 And then the last one is a pretty traditional Catholic funeral service in a church.

11
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
 Cultures really do view death very differently.

12
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,000
 In some cases, it's a celebration of the beginning of another life.

13
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
 If you want to think about the Egyptians, in some cases it's a celebration of a life that's been.

14
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,000
 In some cases, it is about bereavement, about the passing of a person.

15
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:27,000
 But let's look at the more clinical aspects of death, and you do need to understand what each one of these is.

16
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
 For clinical death, we're basically talking about a lack of heartbeat and respiration.

17
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,000
 So that's what you see here in this picture.

18
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:37,000
 This person no longer has a heartbeat, no longer has a respiration.

19
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
 They would be considered clinically dead.

20
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,000
 Now, we bring people back from clinical death all the time.

21
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
 This is when we say people came back from the dead.

22
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,000
 It's normally from clinical death, whereas whole brain death is very different.

23
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,000
 It's a reversible loss of function.

24
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,000
 Basically, the brain has died.

25
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
 We don't see any more EKG wave patterns.

26
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:03,000
 And in our next one, we'll look at the difference between a whole brain death and some of the other ones.

27
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:11,000
 I love this image because what it shows us is the glucose metabolism study.

28
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:16,000
 And people quite often ask me, you know, so we got persistent vegetative state.

29
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:18,000
 We've got whole brain death.

30
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:19,000
 We've got clinical death.

31
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:20,000
 What are they different?

32
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:27,000
 Well, as we already said with clinical death, it's just simply the heartbeat and the lungs have stopped breathing.

33
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,000
 But we can get them back from that.

34
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:34,000
 When we start talking about whole brain death, or we're talking about persistent vegetative state, how do we know this?

35
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:39,000
 It all has to do with the amount of activity that we see within the brain.

36
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:45,000
 So when you look at these three images that we have here, this first image right here,

37
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:48,000
 let me use a different color that didn't show up very well.

38
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:49,000
 Let me use yellow.

39
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
 This first image right here is showing a normal brain.

40
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,000
 Now, how they do this is that they use glucose.

41
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:01,000
 Basically, this PET scan, which is quite commonly used in psychology or in the study of the brain,

42
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:06,000
 is what we do is we have somebody take a pill that has a slight radiation in it.

43
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,000
 What we know is what the brain eats is glucose.

44
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:16,000
 So an active brain like this first one, we would see a lot of lighting up because the brain is active and it's consuming energy.

45
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:25,000
 Whereas in this second image, we definitely see whole brain death because we see no more activity inside the skull.

46
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,000
 It's a very common image when we know that there is whole brain death.

47
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:36,000
 Whereas with a persistent vegetative state, you can see there is some activity, but the activity is very minor.

48
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:43,000
 Basically, what we're going to see is that we only have enough activity to keep the body alive.

49
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:46,000
 And that's how we know that you're in a persistent vegetative state.

50
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:51,000
 So if you ever want to question, "How do we know my aunt or uncle is in a vegetative state?"

51
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:56,000
 They can do a PET scan and they can look at the actual brain activity of the person.

52
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
 Bioethics is something that has become more popular, you might say, in the last 20 years.

53
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:14,000
 As we began to have more ethical questions and decisions about our life and what constitutes a good life and a bad life,

54
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,000
 bioethics is what's really kind of a curve.

55
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:27,000
 Now, bioethics also is something that we look at when we talk about keeping people on ventilators or keeping people in certain living conditions.

56
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:33,000
 Bioethics is basically an offshoot of some court decisions that were made.

57
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:43,000
 There was a young lady named Karen Ann Quinlan, and Karen Ann Quinlan unfortunately took a combination of medications that she was given

58
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:49,000
 and then went and had a drink with some friends and went into a persistent vegetative state.

59
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
 Her parents fought to have her taken off the respirators.

60
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
 We'll talk a little bit more about her later on.

61
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:00,000
 But she's the first one who basically got the right to do that.

62
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:13,000
 And that's sort of where bioethics begins as far as a study is concerned because we do feel that people have a right and an individual freedom here in the United States to make certain decisions about their lives.

63
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,000
 Which brings us to euthanasia.

64
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,000
 Now, we do distinguish euthanasia in the United States into two types, active and passive.

65
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,000
 Now, active is deliberately ending one's life.

66
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:38,000
 Now, one thing we must understand is that in order for it to be euthanasia, it must be the ending of one's life because they have a terminal illness and they're not going to come back from that.

67
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:42,000
 So, not all suicides would be euthanasia.

68
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:49,000
 If I'm perfectly fine body, but I am depressed and decide to kill myself, that is suicide.

69
00:05:49,000 --> 00:06:00,000
 However, if I have a degenerative disease and I'm at the end of that degenerative disease or I have cancer, let's say bone cancer, which is extremely painful.

70
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:15,000
 And I decide that toward the end that I am suffering and decide that I want to eliminate living anymore, then that would be active euthanasia because I am ending my life based on taking something.

71
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,000
 I've made this clear statement, but I also have no way of coming back.

72
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:24,000
 This isn't like I'm going to be able to give back from bone cancer.

73
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:29,000
 Where passive euthanasia is basically my desire not to continue treatment.

74
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:36,000
 Quite often, older people who may have kidney disease decide that they are going to stop dialysis.

75
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:40,000
 Basically, without dialysis, they're going to die.

76
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:49,000
 But they say, "You know what, I'm 89. I'm really tired. I don't want to get this blood done anymore. I'm going to die soon anyway."

77
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:55,000
 And I want my last few months to be pleasant without having to go for dialysis every day.

78
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,000
 So, in that case, they give up available treatment.

79
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:05,000
 A cancer patient who decides not to continue with chemotherapy because they realize at this point they're not going to get better.

80
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,000
 That would be passive euthanasia.

81
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:12,000
 Now, path of euthanasia is legal pretty much everywhere around the United States.

82
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:20,000
 Active euthanasia, that one is a little bit more questionable on if you are allowed to do that or not.

83
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:25,000
 Now, a lot of people will go ahead and do active euthanasia anyway.

84
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:30,000
 Basically, they'll save up medicine, they'll do other things that will help them pass away.

85
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:38,000
 But it isn't legal in all the states, which brings us to physician assisted suicide.

86
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:41,000
 Now, Oregon and Washington were sort of the first two states.

87
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,000
 And I believe there's some more states since then that have done this.

88
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:50,000
 Now, what we talk about with physician assisted suicide is that you had to have a terminal illness.

89
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:55,000
 And basically, it has to be by more than one person who has determined this.

90
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:01,000
 So, it's sort of like one person doesn't make a mistake and tell you you're terminally ill.

91
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:06,000
 And then you go to another person who says, "Oh, no, it's not really that. We can do something for you."

92
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:12,000
 So, it's not so easy to actually get into a physician assisted suicide situation.

93
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:21,000
 So, what we do know is that generally it requires that you have two oral requests and it has to be separated by 15 or more days.

94
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:26,000
 You have to make a written request and you also have to be declared mentally competent,

95
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:32,000
 which means that I can't go in and say, "Hey, grandma's really bad and I think that we should assist her in suicide."

96
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:36,000
 Nope, not going to work. The person has to request it themselves.

97
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:44,000
 Now, some people can request it early on so that as they get worse and they become to the point where they can't perhaps make an oral request,

98
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,000
 the request is already there.

99
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:52,000
 So, again, people with bone cancer may get to the point where they can't even partly speak.

100
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:58,000
 But you also have to give the person the right to self-administer the dose.

101
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:04,000
 Basically, somebody else can mix up the dose for you, but you have to have enough strength to administer to yourself.

102
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:06,000
 And there's all kinds of devices that are used for that.

103
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:14,000
 There's a question bill about that too, as far as are we making people perhaps euthanize themselves earlier

104
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,000
 because they're afraid that they won't be strong enough later on.

105
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:21,000
 But this is not a class to debate those type of things.

106
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:25,000
 More states than Oregon and Washington have allowed these types of situations.

107
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:32,000
 Also, do you understand, because Florida is a very international area with our tourism,

108
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:40,000
 that physician assisted suicide, which is what we call it, is not uncommon in the world.

109
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:48,000
 In fact, we tend to, in the United States, view it much less as an option than other places.

110
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:51,000
 Many countries have very dedicated programs, like we have a hospice program.

111
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:58,000
 They have programs assisted for this type of situation where they have doctors and nurses who are all set up.

112
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:01,000
 They have whole care systems for this.

113
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:07,000
 Some people can, in some countries, they even have locations where, let's say, that I've decided that

114
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:11,000
 I've always wanted to see the ocean or the sunset over the ocean when I died.

115
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:16,000
 And they literally have homes where a person can go and have this.

116
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:21,000
 They almost always have doctors or a physician or a nurse or someone who's on staff there,

117
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:25,000
 so that when the dose is administered, they can make sure that it goes correctly.

118
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:30,000
 Generally, funeral arrangements have already been made to the funeral directors already there.

119
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:35,000
 And the family does little to nothing except for be there for the person who is dying.

120
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:41,000
 And for each other, there's people already around to take care of all the other aspects of that.

121
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
 So in the United States, it's a little bit different.

122
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:49,000
 Don't be surprised if you're dealing in a hospital setting where people are asking for those types of services

123
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:54,000
 if it looks like that person is going to be close to death.

124
00:10:54,000 --> 00:11:02,000
 It is just simply culturally a different way to view death.

125
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:07,000
 Now, what is interesting is that through our lives, we're going to look at death differently.

126
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:12,000
 So when we go from formal operation, and remember, according to Pige,

127
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:15,000
 this is when we have abstract to our post-formal operation.

128
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,000
 This is when we are in our young adult.

129
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:21,000
 We tend to get more emotional about death.

130
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:24,000
 In our formal operation, it's a little bit more abstract.

131
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,000
 We've got the concept of it.

132
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:34,000
 In concrete operational, death is death, and they can sometimes be what we might feel very cold,

133
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:37,000
 but it's not that as much as it's much more black and white.

134
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:38,000
 Dead is dead.

135
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:39,000
 The dog is dead.

136
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:40,000
 That's it.

137
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:43,000
 And in the formal operation, they suddenly go dog is dead.

138
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:44,000
 Do they go to heaven?

139
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
 Do they get all these wonderful things?

140
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
 You know, what happens to them?

141
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:56,000
 And post-formal operational, more of these thoughts of our own death may begin to occur.

142
00:11:56,000 --> 00:12:08,000
 Now, as we get older and we call these death trajectories, different things can help us

143
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:13,000
 sort of begin to deal with one's own death and begin to think about one's own death.

144
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:17,000
 Now, what's interesting is that there's sort of two ways of looking at this.

145
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:22,000
 Diseases such as cancer, which have terminal phases, and we know that death is coming to

146
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:30,000
 us quite often help us as patients or as the person who's dying actually prepare for death.

147
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
 It helps the family prepare for death.

148
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:39,000
 A survey taken not too long ago looked at which ward in a hospital is the least stressful

149
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:40,000
 ward to work on.

150
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:45,000
 And oncology actually turns out to be one of the least stressful for nurses, primarily

151
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
 because it isn't an instant death.

152
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:52,000
 Death people though it's coming, sometimes you get to know the patients, you get to know

153
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:58,000
 the families, and as you work through the process, there's sort of an acceptance of this process

154
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
 and acceptance of the death coming.

155
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:06,000
 Whereas other ones in which the death could occur at any time, heart attack, tend to be

156
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:10,000
 much more stressful for people because when Johnny left, he was perfectly fine, and now

157
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:11,000
 Johnny is dead.

158
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:14,000
 We didn't have any time to prepare for this.

159
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:20,000
 The families are much harder to accept and deal with this because they didn't have that

160
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:21,000
 time to adjust.

161
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:26,000
 It can also be much harder on the nursing staff because these patients are often asking questions

162
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:27,000
 like, "Why did it happen?

163
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
 Could we have done something else?"

164
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:34,000
 Whereas with the cancer or the longer term deaths, we've had that time to sort of work

165
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:41,000
 our way emotionally through those aspects.

166
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:48,000
 Any sort of leaders you might want to say in looking at death is Elizabeth Kubler-Ross.

167
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:53,000
 Now, what's interesting is it depends on sort of what you read about Kubler-Ross and how

168
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:56,000
 old it is on how people view it.

169
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:01,000
 I think our book talks about her stages of dying.

170
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:06,000
 What is interesting is that as she moved on further, it wasn't so much about the staging

171
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:11,000
 and you'll see in the next slide, it's really more about the stages of grief that she talks

172
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:12,000
 about.

173
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:16,000
 The interesting thing that is that up until she came along, nobody really looked at dying

174
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:17,000
 people.

175
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:20,000
 Now, what she did is she did these interviews with terminal patients.

176
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:24,000
 Sometimes they didn't know they were terminal, but what she did find is that there were five

177
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
 distinct emotional reactions.

178
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:32,000
 Quite often, these are taught as if everybody has to go through each one of these in exact

179
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:33,000
 order.

180
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:36,000
 But this is that she did not believe that.

181
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,000
 She did not believe that everybody went through all of these.

182
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
 They didn't go exactly in this order.

183
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:45,000
 What she did produce is what she thought was the most common order.

184
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:50,000
 What I say for the students who are going to go into nursing, learn the order and learn

185
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
 it in a kumbaya way.

186
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:57,000
 Those are something that you do need to know for your exams and nursing.

187
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:02,000
 But in principle, it doesn't mean that it has to happen in the order that we're going to

188
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:03,000
 do.

189
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:11,000
 So, the kublegross grief cycle, which is what it's really much more considered these days,

190
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
 is that we will start with sort of a denial.

191
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:19,000
 And what we're talking about is sort of an informational communication aspect of the grief

192
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:20,000
 process right here.

193
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:25,000
 And that's what we're seeing down here, is that in this section right here, what the

194
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:30,000
 person who is dying or his family members first may be, "No, this is not right.

195
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:33,000
 This can't be true.

196
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:34,000
 I don't believe it."

197
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:38,000
 Now, what we do want is for people to get a second opinion.

198
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,000
 We never want someone to not get a second opinion.

199
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:43,000
 But at the same point, they've got to get out of denial.

200
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:48,000
 Because if they keep denying, then they're not going to be able to work through sort of

201
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:49,000
 this process.

202
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:54,000
 And the second one that she tended to find happen as we're going down in sort of this

203
00:15:54,000 --> 00:16:01,000
 grief cycle is anger, we get frustrated, we get anxious, we're just pissed off.

204
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:05,000
 Then we get down to sort of this area right here.

205
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:09,000
 We call bargaining and we really need more emotional support.

206
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:15,000
 And the bargaining is trying to find meaning and tell one story and make connections.

207
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:19,000
 Quite often, bargaining may also be where you see people saying, "If I can live just

208
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:24,000
 to do this, or if I can just reach this, or I just want to get to my son's graduation,

209
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:27,000
 or I just want to see my daughter get married."

210
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:31,000
 I mean, how many times have you heard about somebody who dies right after or right before

211
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:34,000
 some major event in the family's life?

212
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
 And that's sort of the bargaining phase.

213
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:39,000
 They're helping themselves get there, but they need a lot of emotional support.

214
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:44,000
 That emotional support really does continue through sort of the depression stage.

215
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:50,000
 Now, depression is the one that probably shows up most often in different locations.

216
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:55,000
 Quite often, after we make the bargain, we can get a little depressed.

217
00:16:55,000 --> 00:17:02,000
 Overwhelmingness, hostility, which is why sometimes we really see that bargaining will

218
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:06,000
 kind of fall over here also.

219
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
 But depression is not uncommon.

220
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:14,000
 What Kubo Ross found was that people who were able to get to acceptance.

221
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
 Basically, we explore our options.

222
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
 We're ready to move on.

223
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:22,000
 Not that we're ready to die, but we've kind of accepted that we're going to die.

224
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:28,000
 Then what we're really finding is people who are not satisfied with life,

225
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:31,000
 but they're having an easier time transitioning.

226
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
 And so is the family.

227
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:38,000
 So I always like to tell the story of my friend who died of breast cancer.

228
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
 Unfortunately, she had it twice.

229
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:43,000
 And the first time around, she battled it, fought it.

230
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:46,000
 You have to go five years to be considered to be cancer-free.

231
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:49,000
 Four and a half years in, it comes back.

232
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
 And it came back roaringly.

233
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
 It didn't just a little bit.

234
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:59,000
 So we think it may have been sort of inching back a little bit throughout those four years,

235
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:00,000
 but not caught.

236
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:01,000
 It had gotten up into the brain.

237
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:05,000
 And so this time around, even though she was fighting a very hard battle,

238
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:09,000
 it did come down to the fact that she was not going to make it this time.

239
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
 The cancer was going to be overwhelming.

240
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
 But yet her birthday was coming up.

241
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:17,000
 And so she had accepted that this time she wasn't going to make it through this,

242
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:21,000
 that she wanted to have the quality of life she could have with her children.

243
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:27,000
 And she really did want to try to make it to her son's high school graduation.

244
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:31,000
 But as her birthday came up, it was kind of a question of what do you do?

245
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:32,000
 She's dying.

246
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
 She's not going to be here for a second birthday.

247
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:35,000
 Do we celebrate it?

248
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:36,000
 Do we not?

249
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:37,000
 And so we talked to her.

250
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:40,000
 We said, you know, what would you like to do for your birthday?

251
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:43,000
 Pretty much you get anything you want this year.

252
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:47,000
 And she came up with, I thought, a really interesting thing.

253
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
 And we'll talk about final scenarios.

254
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
 She said she wanted to have a celebration.

255
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:54,000
 She wanted it to be a huge celebration.

256
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
 And the theme was junk food.

257
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
 She said, it don't matter what I eat at this point.

258
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
 And so we had every kind of junk food.

259
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
 You could think we had an ice cream bar.

260
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:03,000
 We had chip bar.

261
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:04,000
 We had candy bar.

262
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:05,000
 We had cakes.

263
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:06,000
 We had pies.

264
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
 I mean, if you named a junk food, it was there.

265
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:11,000
 And we invited everybody and their brother.

266
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:16,000
 What you were to bring, though, was a photo of you and her if you had one.

267
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:19,000
 And a story about that photo.

268
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:24,000
 And if you didn't have a photo of you and her, you were to bring just a written little story

269
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:28,000
 about something she and you had done together.

270
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:34,000
 And when they arrived, the guests, they were all these, I'm going to say, cardboard.

271
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
 But they're things like you have for science projects.

272
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
 And you were to post them on one of those boards.

273
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
 And those boards went to her children.

274
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:45,000
 So that after she died, her children would have all these wonderful stories about her

275
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:47,000
 that she wasn't going to be able to tell.

276
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:50,000
 And we put them sort of in age groups.

277
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:54,000
 Her teens, her twenties, and this on.

278
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:58,000
 Now, this turned out to be one of the most fun things that I could remember doing because

279
00:19:58,000 --> 00:19:59,000
 she had accepted it.

280
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:02,000
 And so people coming in, she knew this was going to be the last time she was going to

281
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:03,000
 see many of them.

282
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
 And for many of them, it was going to be the last time they saw her.

283
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,000
 But it was a very friendly atmosphere.

284
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:10,000
 And lots of people were seeing people they hadn't seen in a long time.

285
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:13,000
 And people didn't stay for, you know, terribly long time.

286
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:14,000
 It's maybe a half an hour.

287
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
 But they were required to eat something junky.

288
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:19,000
 Even the most fit person had to eat something junky.

289
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:22,000
 And we laughed, we cried.

290
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
 It was a wonderful time.

291
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:25,000
 But she had come to acceptance.

292
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,000
 And that's what Kubler-Ross said.

293
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:31,000
 If you can get somebody to that point, if we can help with their emotional support and

294
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
 the guidance, then we get to acceptance.

295
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:38,000
 And sort of, we know this is going to happen, but it doesn't happen out of fear.

296
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
 It doesn't happen out of anger.

297
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:45,000
 It happens sort of with acceptance in this plan to move on.

298
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
 So everybody got some closure that day.

299
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:51,000
 People who had only worked with her a little bit got an opportunity to come by.

300
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:54,000
 People who hadn't seen her in ten years got to come by.

301
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:58,000
 And that is what she's talking about, that if we can get people to acceptance,

302
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:02,000
 that this grief that not only the dying person has, but also their family,

303
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:11,000
 allows that opportunity of, you want the best word I can say is closure.

304
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:16,000
 Which also then brings us to sort of death anxiety.

305
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:19,000
 Most people do have this fear of death anxiety.

306
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:24,000
 And Freud and many psychoanalysts said that really this death anxiety is what tends to

307
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
 motivate us to keep us going.

308
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:33,000
 Because we have this fear, it's a primary motivator of all of our behaviors to try to

309
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:34,000
 keep us from dying.

310
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:37,000
 Now when we're younger, yes, people take much more risks.

311
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:41,000
 And we talked about younger people have less of a version because they think of

312
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
 themselves as having lots of time.

313
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:46,000
 Here's the older we get than the less time we think we have.

314
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:58,000
 It is interesting that men have more fear, but women have more fear of the way they die.

315
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:01,000
 As somebody is dying, as I talked about a little bit with Koop La Ross,

316
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:06,000
 what we know is that people will begin to make decisions about the formal management

317
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:11,000
 of what they want not only with their body, but religious services, things like this.

318
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,000
 People in their head have sort of this final scenario.

319
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,000
 What's everybody going to do once I'm done?

320
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:21,000
 And the older we get, the more likely people will want to talk about that.

321
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
 It can be uncomfortable for others.

322
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:27,000
 But as I always tell people, my mother has threatened me.

323
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:32,000
 She said when she dies, she has to be buried next to her father and not her mother.

324
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:35,000
 And if I put her next to her mother, she's going to come back and haunt me.

325
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:41,000
 So people, they have a way in their head of what they see they want people to do.

326
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:44,000
 And that's sort of this final scenario.

327
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
 So most of us kind of see something now.

328
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
 It does change their lives quite often.

329
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:52,000
 What we see when we're younger may be different than as we get older.

330
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:57,000
 But we do tend to try to do what the person asks.

331
00:22:57,000 --> 00:23:04,000
 So one of the questions is why is it that we try to accommodate this person who is now dead

332
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
 with their final scenario.

333
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:10,000
 And what research found is that the reason that we go out of our way to do these things,

334
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:15,000
 so somebody wants to have their ashes in Alaska.

335
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:17,000
 We find some way to get there.

336
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:23,000
 It's because in a way what we want is for the next generation to do what we want.

337
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:28,000
 So if they can see that we're doing what the generation before wanted,

338
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:32,000
 if I take care of my parents the way my parents have asked me to take care of them,

339
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:35,000
 my children will take care of me the way I've asked them.

340
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:42,000
 And so it becomes sort of this unwritten script that we have this responsibility to adhere

341
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:51,000
 to the person's last wishes if we want our own last wishes to be taken care of.

342
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:56,000
 We do have to talk about, and for most of you already know what hospice is.

343
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:59,000
 Hospice can be done in your own home.

344
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:04,000
 Hospice can be done at a nursing home, hospice can be done in a hospice location.

345
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:09,000
 I've talked to many hospice nurses over the years and they do say it's one of the most

346
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:14,000
 rewarding careers that they've had as long as you have the ability to deal with death.

347
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:17,000
 Because let's face it, these people are going to die.

348
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:22,000
 But that you quite often become acquainted with the family.

349
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:26,000
 There's lots of good feelings of the help that you're giving to these families.

350
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:30,000
 So it is an area of nursing for many of you you might want to look into.

351
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:33,000
 And for those of you who are going to go into health service administration,

352
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:37,000
 it may be an area that you want to look into as far as working as long as you're comfortable

353
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:43,000
 with the concepts of death.

354
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:47,000
 The other thing that your book goes into, and I think this is important that we understand,

355
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:51,000
 are how we make our intentions known for our end of life.

356
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:59,000
 Now a living will is basically a statement of how you are willing to be kept alive.

357
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:02,000
 So I talked a little bit earlier about Karen Ann Quinlan.

358
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:05,000
 Now Karen Ann Quinlan, as I said, was a young woman.

359
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:06,000
 She was in college actually.

360
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:08,000
 She was very, very nervous.

361
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:11,000
 She had gone to her doctor and said I'm having a lot of anxiety and a lot of nerves.

362
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:13,000
 I've moved away from home.

363
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:18,000
 And he, which was rather common at the time, gave her a prescription for tranquilizers.

364
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:21,000
 And so that night she was going to a party and she was very anxious.

365
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:24,000
 So she took the tranquilizers as she was supposed to.

366
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:25,000
 She went to the party.

367
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:28,000
 She had some drinks, didn't get overly drunk, had a few drinks.

368
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:32,000
 But as we now know, tranquilizers and alcohol do not go together.

369
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:36,000
 And unfortunately she went into a vegetative state.

370
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:43,000
 Now her parents fought a great deal because she basically had no brain activity,

371
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:46,000
 but she was being kept alive by machines and tubes.

372
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:49,000
 And they kept saying this is not what Karen would want.

373
00:25:49,000 --> 00:25:50,000
 Karen wouldn't want to live like this.

374
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:51,000
 Karen wouldn't want to live like this.

375
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:56,000
 But yet they weren't allowed to turn off the machines because at that time that was considered

376
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:57,000
 to kill somebody.

377
00:25:57,000 --> 00:25:59,000
 And doctors had no permission to do that.

378
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:04,000
 So they are the ones who went to court who fought for the right to be able to take the machines

379
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:10,000
 off of their daughter with the caveat that if she could breathe on her own,

380
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:12,000
 then of course she would continue to breathe.

381
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:19,000
 But if she didn't breathe, then she would be passing away the way that nature had designed.

382
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:23,000
 So from that moment on, we basically had the concept of living wills.

383
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:25,000
 Now living wills are formal.

384
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:27,000
 You need to get them done formally.

385
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:32,000
 I would always make sure that you have them at all the hospitals and they will always ask about them.

386
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:37,000
 The other one that people don't know a lot about is this durable power for health care.

387
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:41,000
 Now it's a power of attorney for health care in that there's a power of attorney,

388
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:43,000
 and then there's a power of attorney for health care.

389
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:46,000
 They are very different.

390
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:50,000
 For the power of attorney of health care, you're only talking about health care decisions.

391
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:53,000
 Whereas a regular power of attorney, it's all decisions.

392
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:58,000
 So make sure that if you go to do one of these, that you get the one for health care.

393
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:02,000
 My ex-husband was a paramedic.

394
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:09,000
 And I always talk about this because of the many times he's had to deal with this.

395
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:17,000
 Anybody who's got kids, there's a good chance that you do not have this durable power of health care of attorney for your children.

396
00:27:17,000 --> 00:27:21,000
 And you probably don't think much about it because you think, well if something happens to me,

397
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:28,000
 my husband or wife will go ahead and take care of the kids if there's an accident of some sort.

398
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:36,000
 But as he would point out to me, quite often in a car accident, everybody was in the same car, mom, dad, kids,

399
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:41,000
 and the parents die, or one parent dies in the car accident, the other one is completely incapacitated.

400
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:51,000
 And there's nobody available to make decisions for the children because the two adults that are directly related to those kids are incapacitated.

401
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:56,000
 This can delay services to the children.

402
00:27:56,000 --> 00:28:02,000
 If you do have kids, you really might want to think about getting one of these durable powers of attorney for health care

403
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:06,000
 with somebody who is not normally with you all the time.

404
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:13,000
 After 9/11, this was also became an issue because a lot of people were not available.

405
00:28:13,000 --> 00:28:20,000
 So 9/11 happened and people had to go pick up kids and other things like this, and it becomes a little bit of a mess.

406
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:25,000
 We don't think about the fact that we may not always be available for our children.

407
00:28:25,000 --> 00:28:30,000
 You can make these very specific, this is just sort of a general one right here.

408
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:36,000
 You can even have how long before it kicks in, and you can even say exactly what they can and can't decide.

409
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:41,000
 You can have in there when it would actually no longer be valid.

410
00:28:41,000 --> 00:28:47,000
 So in my case when my child was younger, I had one that said that if you could not reach me within 20 minutes,

411
00:28:47,000 --> 00:28:53,000
 that the durable power of health care would kick in, and that once you did reach me,

412
00:28:53,000 --> 00:29:01,000
 that the durable power of health care no longer was valid. So that way if anything had happened to me or I'm at school

413
00:29:01,000 --> 00:29:06,000
 and you're unable to reach me, my child isn't waiting for someone to be able to make this health care decision.

414
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:12,000
 Now the other good thing about this, especially if you have older parents and you can get durable power of attorney for health care,

415
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:17,000
 is that if something does happen to the older parent, you can begin to make some health care decision for them,

416
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:22,000
 but you can also pay their health care bills, which becomes an issue when they don't pay their bills.

417
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:28,000
 So it would allow you to be able to go to the bank, you have your durable power of health care attorney,

418
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:34,000
 and you could show a bill and you could get enough money perhaps from their accounts to pay for those bills or that medication.

419
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:42,000
 But you can be their advocate, especially if they're not thinking quite straight because of certain medications that are kicked in at the time,

420
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:49,000
 but it is something you should look up. I do believe I have a link to one in our school here.

421
00:29:49,000 --> 00:29:53,000
 You don't have to have a lawyer to fill one of these out.

422
00:29:53,000 --> 00:29:58,000
 If you want to talk to a paralegal, that might be good too, but really consider to do that.

423
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:01,000
 Now most people know what a do not resuscitate is, a DNR.

424
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:12,000
 The only thing I do recommend, again this is from having a paramedic as a husband, is that you have to understand that each state has slightly different rules for how DNRs work,

425
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:19,000
 but understand that once a paramedic arrives at your door, if they start resuscitation, they cannot stop.

426
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:23,000
 The only one who can then stop would be the hospital.

427
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:35,000
 So if you have a DNR, you need to make sure that those DNRs are available and can be shown to the paramedic or to whoever arrives at the scene.

428
00:30:35,000 --> 00:30:42,000
 Again, it varies state by state. Some states a photocopy would work. Some states a photocopy does not work.

429
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:45,000
 It has to be the original one that has basically a raised sealed.

430
00:30:45,000 --> 00:30:50,000
 So if you get a DNR, you can get more than one copy at that time with a raised sealed.

431
00:30:50,000 --> 00:30:54,000
 So you might want to get four or five. They're usually not much more money. They're usually like five bucks each.

432
00:30:54,000 --> 00:30:58,000
 You want to make sure they're at their hospital, not only at your home.

433
00:30:58,000 --> 00:31:02,000
 But one of the things that I thought was interesting is that he always said you should have one in your car.

434
00:31:02,000 --> 00:31:09,000
 And you should label in your car where it is, so people at DNRs would have put DNR in glove box.

435
00:31:09,000 --> 00:31:14,000
 And they would look for the DNR, and if it was in there, they would not start the resuscitation.

436
00:31:14,000 --> 00:31:18,000
 But if they didn't see a DNR, even though the person was yelling, "This person has a DNR!"

437
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:21,000
 If they did not see it, they would start the recitation.

438
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:26,000
 And he said he couldn't tell me how many times people were yelling at them because they weren't supposed to do it.

439
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:31,000
 But they didn't have any paperwork that they shouldn't do it, so he had to start.

440
00:31:31,000 --> 00:31:44,000
 So think about that you want to keep them at the locations that you are most likely to be at if you have a DNR or somebody who has a DNR.

441
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:52,000
 As far as grieving and mourning and bereavement are concerned, these three words really do talk about different states.

442
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:56,000
 So bereavement is the state that we're in. Grief is the actual emotion.

443
00:31:56,000 --> 00:32:00,000
 And mourning is basically the way that we show grief.

444
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:06,000
 Now what's mourning and what is interesting is that within a culture, we tend to show grief in the same way.

445
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:09,000
 So I have these three images down here.

446
00:32:09,000 --> 00:32:16,000
 And the first one is after the Dayton, Ohio shooting, and you'll notice that there are lots of flowers and things left by the door.

447
00:32:16,000 --> 00:32:20,000
 We see Colby Bryant after he died, people left flowers and things.

448
00:32:20,000 --> 00:32:26,000
 And then the other one is Princess Diana, and while it's England, England is a lot culturally like us.

449
00:32:26,000 --> 00:32:29,000
 And this is an image after Princess Diana's death.

450
00:32:29,000 --> 00:32:38,000
 So this is sort of a way we show mourning. It's a way we show our grief quite often for our unusual or people is through this process.

451
00:32:38,000 --> 00:32:43,000
 Now this is going to be different for different countries and different locations.

452
00:32:43,000 --> 00:32:49,000
 Again, one thing I was like to point out is that we are a rather international area here in Florida.

453
00:32:49,000 --> 00:32:58,000
 So when we have someone who dies who may not be from this country, understand they may show mourning in a different way, a different way than we show mourning.

454
00:32:58,000 --> 00:33:01,000
 However, grief will be pretty much the same around the world.

455
00:33:01,000 --> 00:33:11,000
 And as you saw, grief generally within six months is where it is the peak, but don't forget, it can last a lifetime.

456
00:33:11,000 --> 00:33:15,000
 So let's talk a little bit more about the grief process and how we cope.

457
00:33:15,000 --> 00:33:21,000
 One of the things that can be really hard is to acknowledge the loss in reality.

458
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:36,000
 And one reason they say the grief is peaks at six months is that that's about when we sort of finally process that everything is done, all the loose ends have been tied up.

459
00:33:36,000 --> 00:33:42,000
 It generally takes us a year for most of us to get through our first grief process.

460
00:33:42,000 --> 00:33:52,000
 The reason we say years, we got to get through that first Thanksgiving, that first Christmas, that first birthday, that first holiday, two years sometimes also is common.

461
00:33:52,000 --> 00:34:04,000
 But one to two years for most people is where our deepest grief and after that, grief may linger off or grief may only happen on specific days.

462
00:34:04,000 --> 00:34:18,000
 So if you had a mother who died, you may feel grief every time you reach her birthday, but on a day to day basis after a while, that grief isn't there.

463
00:34:18,000 --> 00:34:23,000
 So with grief, what we do know is that there's sort of five themes of grief.

464
00:34:23,000 --> 00:34:28,000
 You know, how do we deal with people, how it affects us, how it changes our lives.

465
00:34:28,000 --> 00:34:36,000
 The narrative, I think this one's always interesting, the survival stories about the decrease, the decrease, the deceased.

466
00:34:36,000 --> 00:34:46,000
 So quite often when you have your wakes afterwards, hearing those stories that people talk about and the kind of relationship that was there and the survivors tied to them.

467
00:34:46,000 --> 00:34:51,000
 These are the five sort of themes of grief as we move through that process.

468
00:34:51,000 --> 00:34:56,000
 So those of you who are going to go into psychology, you'll study a lot more about this in your death and dying course.

469
00:34:56,000 --> 00:35:10,000
 For those of you who are in the mental, in the physical health fields, the clinical health fields, understand that these are sort of the five things that you're going to see as you talk to the family members who are left over after somebody dies or is about to die.

470
00:35:10,000 --> 00:35:23,000
 Don't forget grief isn't just after death, because Koopa Ross noticed that people begin to go through the grieving process, even as their loved one is beginning to die.

471
00:35:23,000 --> 00:35:31,000
 Then we're going to look at, and your book sort of then tackles the grief process with each generation.

472
00:35:31,000 --> 00:35:37,000
 Now, this week I have asked you to go back and listen to a podcast that specifically does childhood.

473
00:35:37,000 --> 00:35:41,000
 I really, really encourage you to listen to it.

474
00:35:41,000 --> 00:35:52,000
 You might need a tissue, but it really tackles the issue of grief in childhood because they do go out to the sharing place, which is a grief support group for children.

475
00:35:52,000 --> 00:35:55,000
 And to actually hear children talk about it.

476
00:35:55,000 --> 00:36:07,000
 Now, one of the things that people tend to think is that preschoolers don't understand death, and we do know that they understand death, but they may think of death as being temporary.

477
00:36:07,000 --> 00:36:10,000
 Sometimes you hear them say, "Well, they think death is magical."

478
00:36:10,000 --> 00:36:12,000
 And we don't really mean magical.

479
00:36:12,000 --> 00:36:21,000
 What they may not understand, because remember, if we're talking about preschoolers, these are pre-operational people, the complexities of death.

480
00:36:21,000 --> 00:36:25,000
 So if we've talked about going to heaven, heaven seems magical.

481
00:36:25,000 --> 00:36:27,000
 It's like this magical place.

482
00:36:27,000 --> 00:36:34,000
 We see TV shows where things have gotten hurt and comes right back.

483
00:36:34,000 --> 00:36:38,000
 They may not understand the finality of death is what it is.

484
00:36:38,000 --> 00:36:46,000
 Also, as we know that children who are grieving, they tend to grieve in fits and spurts.

485
00:36:46,000 --> 00:36:59,000
 Meaning is that when you have a five-year-old, the most that they may be able to grieve about that death is five or six minutes, and then they go playing for five or six minutes, and then they grieve again for a little while.

486
00:36:59,000 --> 00:37:04,000
 They don't grieve like adults do, which is constantly and all the way through.

487
00:37:04,000 --> 00:37:13,000
 So as we say, pre-operational, they'll tend to be thinking everyone's sort of thinking the same thing, and they ask you don't understand sometimes why when they say, "Hey, let's go play."

488
00:37:13,000 --> 00:37:18,000
 And you say, "No, I can't. I'm too sad." They don't understand why you're sad because they're not sad at the moment.

489
00:37:18,000 --> 00:37:26,000
 And then a few minutes later, they come out and they're very, very sad, and maybe you're cooking dinner, and they're like, "Why can you be cooking dinner? You should be sad."

490
00:37:26,000 --> 00:37:27,000
 Because they're sad now.

491
00:37:27,000 --> 00:37:37,000
 So it's a little bit harder sometimes for adults for us to go with our kids as far as their patterns concerned because it's more of a fit and stop where the adults are going to be more steady.

492
00:37:37,000 --> 00:37:44,000
 The other thing is that there's a shift between the children are in pre-operational versus the concrete operational thoughts.

493
00:37:44,000 --> 00:37:49,000
 May the concrete operational thoughts are much more black and white. Dead is dead.

494
00:37:49,000 --> 00:37:54,000
 And they'll equate the death of their cat and the death of their father almost in the same way.

495
00:37:54,000 --> 00:38:04,000
 The older we get, generally the better we are at coping things, meaning is that we have a sense of kudola of ourselves in the first place.

496
00:38:04,000 --> 00:38:13,000
 And so we have a better sense of what we may need to do to work through our grief, where younger children may need more time to learn to work through their grief.

497
00:38:13,000 --> 00:38:17,000
 Also, as you will learn, children will regrieve.

498
00:38:17,000 --> 00:38:31,000
 So if my mom, let's say, died when I'm young, then as I hit these milestones in my life, I quite often may regrieve because I would think, "When my mother loved me doing this, I would be so great if my mom was here for this."

499
00:38:31,000 --> 00:38:34,000
 I would really want to tell my mom how I had done that.

500
00:38:34,000 --> 00:38:46,000
 So this regrieving process quite often happens, and people don't always understand why when the mother may have died when they were six or seven, and now they're 26, and they're regrieving.

501
00:38:46,000 --> 00:38:49,000
 They're like, "Basically, I haven't even gotten over it."

502
00:38:49,000 --> 00:38:57,000
 And it's not that as much as they grieve the fact that that person isn't there to experience these things with them, so that grief will come back.

503
00:38:57,000 --> 00:39:02,000
 And remember, we said everybody grieves differently.

504
00:39:02,000 --> 00:39:12,000
 So if we get into adolescents, what we do find, and remember adolescents is between 12 and 20 years old, that most have already experienced some death.

505
00:39:12,000 --> 00:39:19,000
 Either a family member or a friend, quite often a grandparent by now, has died.

506
00:39:19,000 --> 00:39:27,000
 This first experience of death can be severe if it's unexpected.

507
00:39:27,000 --> 00:39:31,000
 If we know a grandparent is sick, we've gotten time to kind of come to it.

508
00:39:31,000 --> 00:39:35,000
 But if it's a sudden death, then that may be much harder for them.

509
00:39:35,000 --> 00:39:46,000
 Especially if they are that younger adolescent, so let's say that 10 to 14 year age, and they lose a parent there.

510
00:39:46,000 --> 00:39:56,000
 What's interesting is not only do they sort of cling to their parents, but their friends now have experienced that death.

511
00:39:56,000 --> 00:40:03,000
 Up until then, I may not have thought about the fact that my parent could die, and now my best friend's parent has died.

512
00:40:03,000 --> 00:40:13,000
 And I may feel grief for not just my best friend's parent, who I know, but I suddenly become very aware of the death of my own parents, and may become a little bit more clingy,

513
00:40:13,000 --> 00:40:17,000
 or a little bit more where you're going, you can't do that, that's dangerous.

514
00:40:17,000 --> 00:40:21,000
 Because I hadn't really thought about the death of their own parent until this occurs.

515
00:40:21,000 --> 00:40:27,000
 So it is very interesting on seeing how people of the younger adolescent age handled this.

516
00:40:27,000 --> 00:40:32,000
 It's also one of the reasons why they have grief counselors at schools after the death of somebody in the school.

517
00:40:32,000 --> 00:40:42,000
 Because the grief that somebody may be feeling may not be about the death of the person who died at school, but may actually be about the fact that they finally understand what death really is.

518
00:40:42,000 --> 00:40:54,000
 Because remember, until we have abstract thought, a lot of this is very iffy, and even the concrete people may not be quite emotionally there, and so it's a little bit harder for them to process.

519
00:40:54,000 --> 00:41:03,000
 But if they do process it, then they may develop a lot of fear all of a sudden about other people dying, because I hadn't really thought about it before.

520
00:41:06,000 --> 00:41:14,000
 In adulthood, we do know that we have a slight difference in viewing it as a young adult versus an older adult.

521
00:41:14,000 --> 00:41:23,000
 As a younger adult, we quite often think that, especially if the person who dies is a younger adult, they've been cheated on life, they had so much more to do.

522
00:41:23,000 --> 00:41:38,000
 We quite often don't feel that way about grandparents who've had a good and long life, because we have accepted for most of us that death is part of life.

523
00:41:38,000 --> 00:41:52,000
 When we see younger people die, or we see somebody maybe even their 30s die, we can feel that that person has been cheated, but losing a partner when you're younger is actually a little bit more difficult than losing your partner when you're older.

524
00:41:52,000 --> 00:42:07,000
 It is this sort of quite often unexpected grief, but also it can take a much longer time for that person to get over that grief, or basically, I don't want to say get over, but move beyond that grief within their lives.

525
00:42:07,000 --> 00:42:29,000
 You may see somebody who's 40 who loses a husband or a wife, and within a year or two, they're out dating again and doing things, whereas you see somebody who's 28 or 31 who loses a partner, they may not be ready to date again for five or six years, because they're still dealing with the grief of that connection with that person.

526
00:42:29,000 --> 00:42:39,000
 With the death of one's own child, meaning is that you're an adult, but your child has died. It's pretty simple. This grief may never grow away.

527
00:42:39,000 --> 00:42:48,000
 This grief and mourning is so intense that we know it quite often lasts a lifetime.

528
00:42:48,000 --> 00:42:59,000
 What we have to also understand is that this grief process not only holds for a child that we have lost, who is alive, but it can also hold for miscarriages.

529
00:42:59,000 --> 00:43:10,000
 It can also hold for stillbirths. It can even happen when you have been given the wrong gender for a child.

530
00:43:10,000 --> 00:43:19,000
 Let's say you went in, you had a sonogram, and you were told that you're going to have a boy.

531
00:43:19,000 --> 00:43:24,000
 In your head, what's happened is you've gone home and you have a whole life planned out for this boy.

532
00:43:24,000 --> 00:43:33,000
 Here's Jack, and when Jack is born, we're going to have all this stuff, and he's going to play T-ball, and then we're going to do this, Jack, and we're going to do that with Jack.

533
00:43:33,000 --> 00:43:44,000
 Then you go back a month later, and they do an x-ray, and they go, "Oh, I guess that was a pinky, not a penis," and Jack is really a girl, and so now Jack has become Emily.

534
00:43:44,000 --> 00:43:49,000
 People don't always understand that there's a mourning for Jack. See, Jack was real in their head.

535
00:43:49,000 --> 00:44:00,000
 Jack was somebody who they were going to have a life with, and it's not that they don't love Emily, and it's not that they don't want a girl, it's just that they've lost Jack.

536
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:10,000
 And so there may be that period of time where they're sad about that, and they're not sad about having a girl, they're sad because they've lost Jack.

537
00:44:10,000 --> 00:44:24,000
 In fact, many people say that once they have in their head sort of had the scenario of who Jack is, all that, that if their second child is a boy, they can't name them Jack, because Jack is gone.

538
00:44:24,000 --> 00:44:32,000
 Jack died in a way. This also happens to parents who are going to adopt a child, by the way. They have this whole adoption all set up.

539
00:44:32,000 --> 00:44:43,000
 They've got this whole thing in their head exactly how it's going to be. They maybe even set up a nursery room, and then the adoption falls through, or the biological parent decides to keep the child.

540
00:44:43,000 --> 00:44:52,000
 They will also go through a mourning period of a loss of a child. You can talk to them ten years later, even if they've adopted another child, they will still talk about the child that they lost.

541
00:44:52,000 --> 00:44:58,000
 Because that was their child in their head. They had this child, they were raising this child, and they want to know what happened to that child.

542
00:44:58,000 --> 00:45:07,000
 So there's still a loss, just like somebody who may have biologically had a child who died in UTRO.

543
00:45:07,000 --> 00:45:16,000
 The death of one's own parent can lead to different types of emotional stress for ourselves.

544
00:45:16,000 --> 00:45:27,000
 It doesn't matter how old you are when your parent dies, what's been lost is sort of this support and guidance system that you think of.

545
00:45:27,000 --> 00:45:34,000
 I've heard 60 year olds who've lost both of their parents, maybe one a little earlier, one a little bit later.

546
00:45:34,000 --> 00:45:38,000
 And when the second parent dies, they'll look at me and say, "I'm an orphan now."

547
00:45:38,000 --> 00:45:46,000
 And you don't think of six year olds as being orphans, but they are because they no longer have any parents alive.

548
00:45:46,000 --> 00:45:52,000
 And there is quite often this struggle of, "What do I do now? Who do I turn to? Where am I going to go?"

549
00:45:52,000 --> 00:45:59,000
 Because no matter how old you are, your parents have always been older and had more experiences and things.

550
00:45:59,000 --> 00:46:10,000
 60 year olds who have 80 year old and 85 year old parents will still talk to them and say, "Hey, what would you think? Blah, blah, blah, blah."

551
00:46:10,000 --> 00:46:17,000
 And that person isn't there anymore. So that loss is very significant, that second parent loss.

552
00:46:17,000 --> 00:46:24,000
 It is interesting that some social scientists have found that men who lose their mothers is the hardest loss of all the different combinations we can think of with parents.

553
00:46:24,000 --> 00:46:30,000
 And that's primarily because men have depended quite often on their mothers as being the emotional support in their life.

554
00:46:30,000 --> 00:46:37,000
 They're the place where I could go and talk about feeling bad about something or feeling anxious, you might want to say.

555
00:46:37,000 --> 00:46:44,000
 I may not want to tell my dad this, I may not want to tell my friends this because that might make me look a little less strong than I am.

556
00:46:44,000 --> 00:46:53,000
 But my mom I could always tell. And so when the mother dies, they've lost perhaps their one emotional support that they had where they felt comfortable to be able to express.

557
00:46:53,000 --> 00:47:00,000
 They've expressed their feelings in a very non-judgmental place.

558
00:47:00,000 --> 00:47:05,000
 It doesn't mean that that daughters don't feel the death of their mothers as much.

559
00:47:05,000 --> 00:47:12,000
 It's just kind of interesting in that it may be actually a little bit harder for the sons to come over that grief.

560
00:47:12,000 --> 00:47:24,000
 This last thing when it talks about Alzheimer's, what social psychologists have also found is that as parents or as a parent goes further into the Alzheimer's disease,

561
00:47:24,000 --> 00:47:32,000
 we as the children of those people quite often begin to see that or view them as not being there anymore.

562
00:47:32,000 --> 00:47:39,000
 That isn't my mother anymore, that isn't my father anymore because the person unfortunately does have such great dementia.

563
00:47:39,000 --> 00:47:52,000
 But then when they die, they actually die, we kind of grieve again because we've already grieved the fact that we've lost who we think of as our parent and now the parent is actually dead.

564
00:47:52,000 --> 00:48:03,000
 So it is an area of social psychology that is studying quite a lot and it does really vary from person to person how we view the death of one's own parent.

565
00:48:03,000 --> 00:48:09,000
 So the age we are as far as the death of one's parent also makes a huge difference.

566
00:48:09,000 --> 00:48:21,000
 For those who are studying psychology, you'll have a whole course called death and dying where you'll study the more interactions and dealing with these sort of issues with your clients.

567
00:48:21,000 --> 00:48:25,000
 That brings us then to late adulthood and notice I didn't say seniors.

568
00:48:25,000 --> 00:48:31,000
 Late adulthood were basically talking about people who now are pretty well established.

569
00:48:31,000 --> 00:48:38,000
 They tend to have less death anxiety primarily because they've experienced death of friends at this point or family members.

570
00:48:38,000 --> 00:48:45,000
 It's very rare for someone to get to be an older adult and not have had some sort of an experience with death within their community.

571
00:48:45,000 --> 00:48:52,000
 Whether it is a family member, a fellow co-worker, even people in the community.

572
00:48:52,000 --> 00:49:00,000
 Coping skills quite often have been developed at this point and the coping skills are how to deal with the feelings they may have,

573
00:49:00,000 --> 00:49:02,000
 have been developed.

574
00:49:02,000 --> 00:49:11,000
 So we have this ability to accept it, not only that but as we get older we begin to accept that death is part of the landscape of life.

575
00:49:11,000 --> 00:49:15,000
 There is birth and death and everything in between you might want to say.

576
00:49:15,000 --> 00:49:24,000
 So it is sort of more that acceptance of death happens not so much my death as much as death within life happens.

577
00:49:24,000 --> 00:49:34,000
 The death of one's own child or grandchild in late hood though is handled a little bit differently.

578
00:49:34,000 --> 00:49:42,000
 The biggest problem that you might want to say that we have to deal with, those of you who are going to psychology especially,

579
00:49:42,000 --> 00:49:51,000
 and those who are going into the medical field is that when there's a death of a grandchild the grandparent quite often hides their grief.

580
00:49:51,000 --> 00:49:56,000
 Because their primary focus is on their child. Their child is still alive.

581
00:49:56,000 --> 00:50:04,000
 Their child is grieving the death of the grandchild and so they don't want to bring any more stress to their own child.

582
00:50:04,000 --> 00:50:13,000
 So if they don't have a safe haven to be able to express this grief, if they don't have some place where they can express and bring this grief out,

583
00:50:13,000 --> 00:50:18,000
 it quite often can affect them negatively as far as their physiological concern is there.

584
00:50:18,000 --> 00:50:26,000
 So if you happen to have a neighbor who you hear, their grandchild has died, perhaps you can spend a few minutes with them,

585
00:50:26,000 --> 00:50:37,000
 so they have someone to talk to about this because they've hidden it quite often from their own family, especially from their own child.

586
00:50:37,000 --> 00:50:46,000
 Well, that brings us to the end of this course. So if you have finished listening to this, hopefully you will go on to the last week of class.

587
00:50:46,000 --> 00:50:50,000
 There are student evaluations generally for you to take. Please take those.

588
00:50:50,000 --> 00:50:57,000
 And then you can celebrate and reflect because you're just about ready to take exam for the last exam of the class.

589
00:50:57,000 --> 00:51:03,000
 I hope you've enjoyed these. And if you need me, there's my email address as usual.

